Read the folder and report what is wrong; writes nothing.

\e[1mUsage\e[0m: \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mcheck\e[0m \e[36m[OPTIONS]\e[0m

\e[2mAlso typed as \e[0m\e[1m\e[36mink c\e[0m

\e[1mExamples\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mcheck\e[0m           \e[2m# \e[0mRead this folder and list its problems
  \e[1m\e[36mink\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mcheck\e[0m \e[1m\e[36m--strict\e[0m  \e[2m# \e[0mThe same, but a warning is an exit 6

\e[1mOptions\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --strict\e[0m  Exit with 6 when there are warnings

\e[1mSource\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --source\e[0m \e[36m<KIND>\e[0m  `\e[1m\e[36mfolder\e[0m` reads the files on disk, `\e[1m\e[36mgit\e[0m` reads a commit
                       \e[2m[default: `git` when .git exists, said as a notice]\e[0m
  \e[1m\e[36m    --rev\e[0m \e[36m<REV>\e[0m      The commit to read, as `\e[1m\e[36mgit\e[0m \e[1m\e[36mrev-parse\e[0m` takes it; only for
                       a git repository \e[2m[default: HEAD]\e[0m

\e[1mNotes\e[0m
  The folder is read and interpreted exactly as a build would; nothing is
  written.

\e[1mExit codes\e[0m
  0  Done; warnings, if any, were printed
  1  The site has errors
  2  Wrong usage, or a folder that is missing; nothing was changed
  6  --strict was given and there were warnings

